<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

!! K15 STEP 0 — custody contract before bytes move


''Status:'' build specification, 2026-10-19. ''LEAD'' marks a target disposition or fact whose payload, selector, or implementation has not been proved by the cited code. Source paths below are relative to `/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine`; line numbers identify the read-only snapshot. No suite, build, or git write in that repository forms evidence for this specification.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/law-and-acceptance-boundary`>>

!!! Law and acceptance boundary


Every persisted custody carrier and every act that touches it has exactly one target class. ''D'' dissolves confidential material into rows owned by one PersonaGroup and sealed with that group's face custody key (FCK). ''F'' carries deliberately public material or ciphertext a blind floor may copy; it has no custody decryption key. ''S'' carries a *named* node-only confidential row under a named node principal and opener. A shared verb creates no shared key. A mixed carrier splits before it receives a class. The browser has no vessel custody key (S key). A node has no residual omnibus key: only an actual S row can justify a node custody door. Current `hot`/`cold` mean S-sealed, not target ownership (`vault-carriers.ts:5-22,64-68`). These are operator rulings recorded at `stable-founding-road.mem:205-207`.

The face identifier in this document means a stable, canonical PersonaGroup identifier. Current `h<N>` is a local handle index, not sufficient by itself as a cross-vessel cryptographic identity. ''LEAD:'' migrations map each current handle to one authenticated PersonaGroup before assigning an FCK or moving bytes; an ambiguous map refuses the migration. A row with no provable owner refuses rather than falling into F or S.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/row-census-node`>>

!!! Row census — node


This table covers every `buildTable()` row in `packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:236-398`. “Current” names the code's custody class. “Owner / target” is the K15 contract. Where the source only names a filename, ''LEAD'' requires a payload and caller audit before the classification can be implemented. There is no implicit S catchall.

| Current row and code line | Current | Owner / target and required cut |
| `vessel-key` `:240-242` | floor | ''S:'' private place signing seed owned by this node. Its public verifying key belongs in F. The scoped signer grants five framed floor duties; it is never a custody S key or face ancestor. `node-vessel-identity.ts:429-442,451-487` proves the private seed is read from disk and used at the floor door. |
| `relay-gate-seed` | floor | S: random durable relay service key; the relay-only opener serves the handshake, and explicit rotation requires hearth address re-pinning. |
| `vessel-kel` `:243-245` | floor | ''F:'' public armed chain (inception and rotations), node place principal. |
| `vessel-rotating` | floor | ''S:'' the staged replacement signer and its signed rotation, present only between a rotation's stage and publish. |
| `s-slots` `:246-248` | floor | ''D/S split:'' the node S tree wraps one random S key and seals `gate-root` alone. Each face has its own FCK tree, and no face FCK wraps under a node key. |
| `sk-slots` `:249-251` | floor | ''S conditional, node:'' retire current S-key-to-SK wrapping. No serving row exists today (`:12-14`). A future serving payload gets its own named S key and boot rule before this tree survives. |
| `fk-slots` `face-custody.ts` | floor | ''D (the face's own tree):'' one tree per face at `faces/<fcid>/fk-slots.bin`, wrapping that face's random custody key under each human route that face asked for. No S key wraps it and no sibling face's route opens it; the face's rows and this tree rest in one directory, so a bundle or a removal moves them together. |
| `face-index` `face-index.ts` | floor-plain | ''F:'' the one floor row that names the faces a node can unlock before any face opens: the count of faces (tombstones included), each group's digest, each founding ask's digest and each random directory name, and no other byte. It routes and never decides; see #/the-floor-index. |
| `vessel-card` `:254-256` | retired | A stored Keyhive place ContactCard refuses the boot by name; the public place founding card and KEL remain F. |
| `charter` `:257-259` | floor-plain | ''F:'' signed public founding charter. |
| `carried-charter` `:260-262` | floor-plain | ''F:'' signed public carried charter. |
| `carriage-consent` `:263-265` | floor-plain | ''F:'' public consent/pin as the row comment defines it. |
| `nexus-key-line` `:266-268` | floor-plain | ''F:'' public hand-over keys/signatures. |
| `carriage-admit` `:269-271` | floor-plain | ''F:'' public gate reach/pin. |
| `sealed-rest` `:275-277` | floor-plain | ''F carriage:'' sealed lockboxes, signed records and indexes remain opaque transport; cryptographic content ownership stays with the relevant face's Keyhive/content-key seat. The row is not an FCK escrow. |
| `sealed-own` `:279-281` | floor-plain | ''F only for proved opaque pointers/marks. LEAD:'' audit names and values for face-linking metadata; any private index dissolves into D. |
| `sealed-chunk` `:283-285` | floor-plain | ''F carriage:'' Keyhive ciphertext under opaque addresses; its content key and identity remain D. |
| `gate-root` `:288-289` | hot | ''D:'' the scoped node gate secret serves the place's sovereign gate bundle; it derives no face identity. |
| `switch-acts` `switch-act-log.ts` | hot | ''D:'' the vessel's own ring of session-switch acts (`identity/switch-acts.json`), sealed under the device key: each act names a causal position and the two faces a session left and took, so it rests on this vessel alone and in no document a face's fleet syncs. The ring keeps the newest 256 acts and never heads with an outcome whose intent left; a malformed row restarts the ring at a `reset` act naming why. |
| `place-archive` `:290-291` | retired | A stored vessel Individual Archive refuses the boot by name; each PersonaGroup keeps its own veil archive. |
| `veil-archive` `:292-293` | face-hot | ''D:'' the node rests each face's archive at `veil-archive-g<digest of its PersonaGroup>`, so no free index selects it; its FCK arrives with the K15.6 fence. |
| `face-atom` `face-atom-store.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the face's random atom seed, one per (vessel, PersonaGroup), the device edge's delegate and countersigner and the face's Keyhive Individual. The row's file name carries the SHA-256 digest of its PersonaGroup, so no clear file names a face, and the face-atom custody contract of `@lararium/mesh` (`face-atom-custody.ts`) holds stand, read and keep: absent mints, the same bytes read as a no-op, other bytes refuse as `FaceAtomConflict`. It rests under that face's FCK and never rides a bundle, a wire or a board. |
| `face-atom-ask` `face-atom-store.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the seed a founding rests before its PersonaGroup exists, named by the digest of the founding's own ask (hearth true name, binding mode, mount flag and compartment); the group row keeps the seed once the group stands and the ask row retires with the bootstrap entry. Same key class and fence as `face-atom`; it never rides a bundle. |
| `face-owner` `custody-holder.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the face's own label, sealed under its custody key: its custody id and the digest of its PersonaGroup. After a face unlocks, the holder compares this row with the floor index entry and refuses by name on a mismatch before it seats anything. Never rides a bundle. |
| `enroll-pending` `:295-296` | hot, reserved | ''D, LEAD:'' if materialized, bind each pending enrolment to its target face; reject a row with no target. There is no current writer. |
| `grant-pending` `:297-298` | hot, reserved | ''D, LEAD:'' split per granting/target face and bind the authority relation; reject an unassigned pending secret. There is no current writer. |
| `admissions` `:299-300` | hot, reserved | ''D, LEAD:'' confidential admission state partitions by owning face; public signed proof may be F after payload audit. There is no current writer. |
| `anchors` `:301-302` | face-hot | ''D:'' face-indexed anchor lines and private identity state under that face. Current `bundle: lines` is an export selector, not key separation. |
| `persona-roster` `node-vessel-identity.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' each face keeps its own root line at `faces/<fcid>/.persona-roster-h<N>.json`, selected by handle index (= ordinal). No shared roster stands: the floor index names the faces, and a locked `persona list` prints those public indices alone. `bundle: lines` carries the one line that names the face's root. |
| `persona-petnames` `node-vessel-identity.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' per-face private name in the face's own row (`.persona-petnames-h<N>.json`); it appears only after that face unlocks. |
| `persona-declarations` `node-vessel-identity.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' each face's private declaration (handle, seat claim, chair) in its own row (`.persona-declarations-h<N>.json`). A signed, intentionally public declaration travels by its own published record and never by this row. |
| `public-handles` `node-vessel-identity.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the owner mapping of a face's own published record in its own row (`.persona-public-handles-h<N>.json`); public handle bytes ride their own signed record. |
| `vessel-face-key` `face-key-custody.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the private face-scoped vessel key rests in the local face whose proof key the scope names, `faces/<fcid>/vessel-face-key-h<N>-<scope digest>.bin`; the founding or join that minted it binds it, and a reader finds it by the scope digest across the face directories (names alone) before that face's key opens it. It stays local, outside a face bundle. |
| `face-address-secret` `fleet-join-custody.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the face's fleet address secret rests in that face, `face-address-secret-g<sha256 of its PersonaGroup>.bin`; the name's digest is the group digest the floor index holds. Local unless a defined face recovery route includes it. |
| `fleet-join-pending` `fleet-join-custody.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' one pending join per target face, `fleet-join-pending-g<group digest>.json` in that face (its X25519 secret and request); the joiner founds the face's custody at its request, so a pending join with no face refuses by name. |
| `face-join-record` `fleet-join-custody.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the joined face's private record and grants, `face-join-record-g<group digest>.json` in that face. |
| `handle-book` `node-circle-store.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' each face keeps its own recognition book in its own row (`.handle-book-h<N>.json`): the nyms it recognised and the private labels it keeps for them. A recognition reads and writes the book of the face the command acts as (`--face N`, else the one face standing open); a face whose root this vessel does not hold recognises nobody, and a flat `.handle-book.json` reads as an earlier shape (refused by name, with the fresh-start runbook). `bundle: whole` carries the face's book. |
| `recovery-contact-set` `:337-338` | face-hot | ''D:'' each face's recovery contact/recovery commitments under that face; its intended local row rides its one-face bundle. Recovery contacts keep their own secret shares. |
| `face-provisional` `:341-342` | face-hot | ''D:'' local provisional restore state for that face; it does not ride a bundle. |
| `hosting-state` `hosting-store.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the hosting face is the face whose hosting leaf authorizes the service. Its state rests in that face, `hosting-h<N>/<aid digest>/state.json`; the Nexus AID proves no face, so the binding is the face's own directory for that AID (never the floor index), found by directory name across the face directories. The first roll founds it under the face it names (`--face N`), else the one face standing open, and refuses by name (`FaceNotNamed`) where several stand and it named none. |
| `hosting-spent` `hosting-store.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' per-face replay and spend authority beside its state, `hosting-h<N>/<aid digest>/spent-*`. |
| `hosting-carry` `hosting-carry.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the hosting face's rhythm records and the guests' sealed ciphertext rest in that face, `hosting-h<N>/<aid digest>/carry/...`; the walker, a different face, owns the inner ciphertext's content key, and no guest key lands at the host. |
| `walk` `node-walk-store.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the walking face owns its private walk record, `walk-h<N>-<gate digest>.json` in that face; a vessel still walks at one hearth (every face's names are read), and the daemon lists only the unlocked faces' walks. |
| `gate-memory` `gate-memory-rows.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' a gate's withdrawal memory rests in the face whose gate learned it, `gate-memory-h<N>-<subject digest>.bin`; each gate reads and writes only its own face's rows, and another unlocked face's withdrawal reads absent through it. |
| `transition-pending` `seal-ceremony.ts` | floor-plain | ''F:'' field audit: a crossing in progress holds public key-sets, signatures and witnesses and no secret, so it rests in the clear in the seal home (`plain-rows.ts`), readable with no key, written under the custody claim. |
| `transitions` `seal-ceremony.ts` | floor-plain | ''F:'' field audit: each sealed crossing record is a verified public record (key-sets, signatures, witnesses), so it rests in the clear beside the charter and verifies from the charter alone. |
| `cofound` `cofound-record.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the acting face's record of a joint founding, `cofound-h<N>-<offer fingerprint>.json` in that face; the jointly signed image is public and rides the offer and join files. |
| `outbox-key` `outbox-key.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' one outbox key per sending face, `outbox-key-h<N>.bin` in that face. A flat `outbox-key.bin` reads as an earlier shape. The parcels partition by the sending face's key (a parcel sealed under another face's key never opens). |
| `sealed-outbox` `sealed-store-fs.ts` | outbox | ''D:'' parcels seal under the sending face's own outbox key (`outbox-key-h<N>`), so a parcel sealed under another face's key never opens; wire ciphertext copied as F carriage is separate. The singleton parcels an earlier shape wrote under the one outbox key refuse by name. |
| `persona-root` `:377-378` | face-cold | ''D:'' face root under that face's FCK; one-face bundle includes it. |
| `face-proof-key` `:381-382` | face-cold | ''D:'' per-face Nexus proof key under that FCK; bundle selection remains face-specific. |
| `seat-key` `:386-387` | face-cold | ''D:'' face's local seat key; stays on its vessel, not in a face bundle. |
| `cadence-key` `cadence-key-store.ts` | face-cold | ''D:'' the minting face owns the hearth cadence key, `cadence-key-h<N>-<digest>.bin` in that face; a reader finds it by the digest across the face directories. No vessel S principal in this lane. |
| `restore-pending` `:394-395` | cold | ''D:'' one target-face restore transaction; split the global file, authenticate its target before staging, and never stage sibling material. |
| `device-share` `:396-397` | face-cold | ''D:'' face-indexed recovery device share, not a common device share. |

The mounted face is the composition's own state: no `active-persona` row rests (the person names the face the composition mounts at each unlock, `lares vault unlock --face N`), and no command reads it as a default face.

Rows whose class includes ''LEAD'' block byte movement until their payload and owning principal are proved. Current `face` and `bundle` fields describe filename/export selection only (`vault-carriers.ts:119-165`); they never confer cryptographic separation. The existing S-key rotation sweeps all hot/cold rows (`:64-68,477-510`), so K15 replaces it with per-face and named-S rotation.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/row-census-browser`>>

!!! Row census — browser


The exact `CUSTODY_ROW` alternatives are at `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-custody.ts:54-68`. They currently share one `custody-slots` tree and one page-held S key for sealed writes (`:2-12,95,386-402`). `catalog-url` is explicitly outside that matcher and public (`:5-13`). The target has no browser S key and no browser S.

| Current `CUSTODY_ROW` alternative | Target class, principal and split |
| `vessel-key` | ''F and place KEL seed:'' the public verifying key routes the browser vessel; its private seed signs the place KEL alone and grants no face identity. |
| `keyhive-archive` | ''Retired:'' a clear or sealed vessel Individual Archive refuses browser boot by name; the selected face keeps its own `veil-archive-g<PersonaGroup>` row. |
| `veil-archive-g<PersonaGroup>` | ''D:'' the browser rests the face atom's Archive under its PersonaGroup; the node rests the corresponding row under a digest of that name. |
| `persona-root-h<N>` | ''D:'' that PersonaGroup root. |
| `persona-root-roster` | ''D/F split:'' roots, private labels and membership D per face; minimum deliberately public routing F only after leakage review. |
| `nexus-key/h<N>/*` | ''D:'' that face's private per-Nexus keys; public verification line F if separately represented. |
| `vessel-face-key/*|*` | ''D:'' the face named by authenticated relation, never inferred from delimiter text alone; add owner lookup/binding. |
| `face-address-secret/*` | ''D:'' that face's address secret, the one its working documents' addresses derive from; it rides the face's bundle and a fleet join's answer, and lands under the face's own row; add authenticated owner mapping. |
| `fleet-join-pending` `fleet-join-custody.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' one pending join per target face, `fleet-join-pending-g<group digest>.json` in that face (its X25519 secret and request); the joiner founds the face's custody at its request, so a pending join with no face refuses by name. |
| `walk/*` | ''D/F split, LEAD:'' private face receipt/relationship D; proved blind ciphertext carriage F. |

The browser's clear-before-slot allowance for `vessel-key` and `veil-archive-g<PersonaGroup>` (`browser-custody.ts:writeKeystoreRowHeld`) does not apply to target D. Every face secret needs its own slot tree before first persistence, including first fleet join (`stable-founding-road.mem:200`). `tm-clear-password` currently clears the common tree and rows (`browser-custody.ts:34-39,613-650`); target clear/removal acts name one face and cannot clear another.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/key-classes-and-routes`>>

!!! Key classes and routes


Add this row beside the key table at `bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store.mem#/keys` (`:60-73`):

| Key | Who holds it | What it seals | Where it rests | What unlock needs | Removal and recovery |
| ''Face custody key (FCK)'' | One PersonaGroup's admitted, unlocked face session; on browser, only the authorized tab's sealer holds bare key material | That face's custody rows: root, Keyhive/Veil archive, face proof/member and recovery material, face-private roster/book/join/outbox partitions. It does not replace per-bag content keys (`sealed-store.mem:65-72`). | Fresh random key, wrapped only in that face's independent slot/recovery tree; bare only during authorized live use. Never wrapped by S key, a node S key, another face, or a common Keyhive archive. | That face's passphrase, paper route, or measured PRF route; each browser tab asks for its own face unlock. A node face act separately proves its face. | Removing a face revokes its slots/admission and retires its local rows without rotating siblings; old offline bundles and already learned plaintext remain readable to their holders. A one-face recovery route restores only that face's FCK and rows. |

The per-face slot tree carries wrapped FCK, slot identifiers and nonsecret KDF parameters; it cannot embed a sibling wrapper. Bind/unbind/rotation prove the standing FCK and affect that tree and that face's rows alone. The bundle contains only intended wrapped recovery routes, not a bare FCK or recovery-contact-held secret shares. A passkey/PRF slot stands only after its derivation is measured on target devices (`browser-custody.ts:41-43`). Last browser tab/session close drops bare face keys and returns to F (`stable-founding-road.mem:199,202`); an installed app or worker does not extend authority.

''Relay service S row.'' `relay-gate-seed.hex` rests under the identity home as a random 32-byte floor seed. The relay service owns it; `openRelayGateSeed` alone opens it for the relay handshake. `rotateRelayGateSeed` replaces it and hands back the new public pin for hearth re-pinning after restart. It grants no place signer duty, face opening, daemon authority or content read.

''Node S register.'' The node's named place signing row opens through `vesselPlaceSignerDoor`: `proveStandingKey` holds the private seed to the KEL head, and the door lends only five framed floor duties. `vesselFloorKeyDoor` alone lends registered floor children. The random `relay-gate-seed` row belongs to the relay service, opens only by `openRelayGateSeed`, and rotates by explicit `rotateRelayGateSeed`; its public address pin changes and hearths re-pin. Neither row is a face ancestor or a custody S key. The SK tree has zero serving payload rows. A container inherits these named rows and mints no separate service authority.


<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/carrier-binding-and-overwrite`>>

!!! Carrier binding and overwrite


''LEAD target wire format:'' each D seal authenticates one canonical context containing a format/domain name and version, vessel or store namespace, immutable PersonaGroup ID, row name, row kind, and the complete logical row reference. Encode fields unambiguously as length-prefixed bytes, analogous to the sealed value context at `sealed-store.mem:85-106`. Supply the expected context from the authenticated row selector on open; never trust a ciphertext's claimed face. A carrier copied into another face's slot, a different row name, or a different kind fails authentication. F and S use distinct domain names; an S row binds its named node principal and row kind. New names/formats refuse old ciphertext rather than treating it as clear. ''LEAD:'' exact byte layout and migration version receive implementation review before K15.6; these fields and refusal behavior are normative now.

Generalize `writeSealedCarrier` from `(key, tree, carrier)` to an explicit `(row class, owner, key, matching tree/door, bound carrier context)`. The caller proves that the key opens the *same owner and row class* against its standing slot tree; an S writer proves its named node door; F has no sealed-carrier writer. Preserve the existing exclusive act, absent-or-authentically-opened standing row rule, and refusal without replacement (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-288`). Seal a full caller-supplied replacement with the same context, write a sidecar, read it back byte-identical, authenticate/open it, then rename (`:290-304`). A failed old-row open, class mismatch, tree mismatch, stale/ambiguous migration shape or failed readback leaves the occupied carrier byte-for-byte unchanged. A whole archive replacement is not an implicit read-modify-write. No encrypt-only capability can perform this overwrite proof. The matching-key rule also governs delete and rotation; never use A's key or node S to authenticate B's standing row.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/one-face-bundle`>>

!!! One-face bundle


A bundle for PersonaGroup X contains X's root/identity, X's Keyhive and Veil archive, X's selected proof/recovery keys and rows, X's recovery contact set/commitments, intended wrapped FCK recovery route, X's selected private roster/book lines, and X's content-key memory section and eligible history. It is sealed under the bundle key and its own route (`sealed-store.mem:68`; `vault-carriers.ts:123-165,301-342,377-397`). It never contains a bare FCK, another face's slot or row, another face's identifiers or content keys, a node place signer, a node S key, recovery-contact-held secret shares, or local-only vessel seat/outbox state. `bundle: stays` remains local unless an explicit per-face recovery rule supersedes it. Restore X into a fresh vessel without opening Y, and refuse an ambiguous old mixed archive. Restore does not mint authority over a sibling.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/reds-executable-acceptance-statements`>>

!!! Reds — executable acceptance statements


# ''Isolation red, every row:'' found A and B with independently random FCKs/trees, occupied D carriers for both, and F plus any named node S values. Unlock A alone. Bypass routers, sessions, UI and store adapters; try direct authenticated open, occupied-row replacement, delete, rotation, backup and restore on *each* B carrier using every A key (FCK, member, identity, content, outbox, recovery), all floor values, and all node S/S key legacy values. Every B operation refuses cryptographically and leaves each B occupied ciphertext byte-for-byte unchanged. Copy A ciphertext into B's slot and swap row name/kind: authentication fails. A's same-row replacement succeeds. Restore A's bundle on a fresh vessel: B identity, key, archive and identifiers are absent; B stays unopened. Remove A: B and F still work. This red is stronger than owner-route refusal (`stable-founding-road.mem:459`; `sealed-writer.ts:279-304`).
# ''Browser desktop / laptop:'' two A tabs and one B tab each ask independently; A-caused seal reaches its admitted asker. If that asker cannot answer, only another admitted unlocked A tab may seal; otherwise work names `seal-awaits-session`. Last tab closes, all live FCKs vanish and F remains. A cannot open B even by bypassing holder routing (`holder-routes.ts:23-31`; `shared-holder-core.ts:117-125`; `stable-founding-road.mem:199,202,205`).
# ''iOS phone / Home Screen app:'' suspend or kill the asker during an occupied write. The write waits or completes from an eligible same-face session; it never uses a sibling or infers death from silence. Relaunch starts at F and asks for that face again. ''LEAD:'' device lifecycle must be exercised on the target browser (round 4's YIN handback in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face`>>).
# ''Android phone / WebAPK:'' kill worker/process and restart; F reads, no D opens before that face unlocks, and A's former worker state cannot open B. Install mode adds no key authority. ''LEAD:'' exercise on target device (round 4's YANG handback in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face`>>).
# ''Tablet waking floor:'' unattended public/ciphertext sync succeeds; direct D decrypt or transformation fails until that face's session is admitted. The open product requirement for unattended *private* sync cannot silently borrow FCK (`stable-founding-road.mem:207`).
# ''Native desktop shell, LEAD:'' keychain/biometric, if used, opens only the selected face's FCK. A second account/session and process restart cannot open A or B without their routes; F remains.
# ''Probe / Mudlet seat:'' with A admitted, request B through the node and by direct local ciphertext open. Both refuse; the probe retains no FCK or node S. ''LEAD:'' probe adapter implementation is outside this carrier census.
# ''Node daemon / hearth:'' boot with no face admission and no omnibus key; F serves. Admit A and run the isolation red over every B node carrier, including archive, roster, pending join and outbox. Lock/restart drops volatile face keys. Hearth composition grants no extra row principal. Separately verify the place signer only under whichever OPEN JOINT option is ruled (`vault-carriers.ts:239-398`; `node-vessel-identity.ts:429-487`).
# ''Herm / crossroads:'' zero face rows and zero current serving rows; public card/relay service works before any face. No private seed leaks to output. Reboot drops raised ephemeral caps. The place signer's boot behavior follows the OPEN JOINT ruling, not an assumed F classification (`vault-carriers.ts:12-14,240-256`; `stable-founding-road.mem:207`).
# ''CLI:'' each run proves its own face; A's prior node admission cannot authorize a B run. No credential/key bytes enter receipts or logs (`stable-founding-road.mem:201`).
# ''MCP / watcher:'' each invocation/session proves its face; restarting client transport does not inherit A or authorize B. B succeeds only after B admission (`stable-founding-road.mem:201`).
# ''Container node, including harbor/civic/mesh scenarios:'' restart unattended with persistent storage. Public F service starts; all D and any door-gated S refuse until explicit authorized unlock. No image, environment, health endpoint or restart policy supplies a credential. Place signer follows the unresolved option (`stable-founding-road.mem:207`).
# ''Guest island / Unreal host, LEAD:'' hostile guest with A context cannot open host B or guest B. If guest persists its own keys, repeat the full isolation red against its *independent vessel* store and never inherit host FCK (`stable-founding-road.mem:207`).
# ''Generic worker host / LarVessel:'' giving a worker A's context and bypassing its router still fails to open or replace B; worker lifetime changes availability only. Public floor and ciphertext carriage continue without face keys.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/build-order-and-dependencies`>>

!!! Build order and dependencies


''K15.0, this gate:'' freeze the exhaustive node/browser row census, prove each split and owner selector, define FCK/tree/recovery and carrier context, specify old-shape refusal and the isolation red. No identity or custody bytes move. ''K15.1:'' use the existing two-hive same-process isolation probe, then give each face its own store, registry, archive and key seat. ''K15.2:'' make the face the sole identity/group owner; capture two-face event bytes and reject any shared creator/issuer. ''K15.3:'' route sessions and registries by exactly one face and land ''F6 slice three'' asker sealing with the correct row-class key and occupied-row proof. ''K15.4:'' separate content-key rolls and removal by face, including K12 fresh group behavior. ''K15.5:'' one-face restore and a herm that carries without face authoring. ''K15.6:'' one migration fence for identity bytes, split stores, FCK slot trees, archives and custody formats; refuse old mixed archives by name. This follows `stable-founding-road.mem:459,507`. ''B1'' consumes browser no-S key, per-tab face asks, slot-before-first-secret and last-tab floor (`:199-202,206-207`). F6 routing does not precede the key boundary.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-floor-index`>>

!!! The floor index of faces (ruled 2026-10-23)

A node with no omnibus key still has to name the faces it can unlock before any face opens, so the identity home keeps ONE F row, `face-index`, in the clear. It lists the node's faces in local face order. Each entry holds the SHA-256 digest of its PersonaGroup (null while the founding stands), the digest of the founding's ask (null once the group stands) and the filename of the face's own slot tree. It holds no name, no handle, no prefix, no label and no key bytes.

''The accepted leak, and only it:'' the count of the node's faces, tombstones included (so a removal history leaks as a count); each PersonaGroup's digest; each founding ask's digest; and each tree's random filename. Nothing else: no name, no handle, no prefix, no label, no key byte, and nothing derived from the face custody id beyond the filename itself. The digests already name the carriers on disk (`face-atom-g<digest>.bin`, `veil-archive-g<digest>.bin`), so the index shows a stolen disk no fact it does not already show. A removed face leaves a tombstone entry, so no other face's local ordinal moves.

''The index routes and never decides.'' It rests at F, unauthenticated, so a swapped or tampered entry could point a group at another face's tree. After an unlock, the D anchors row under that face's own FCK names its PersonaGroup; the vessel compares that group with the index entry's digest and refuses by name on a mismatch, before it seats anything. The index never chooses a worn face's authority either: `readFace` reads the sealed anchor.

''The owner in every D context is the face custody id (`fcid`),'' a random 16-byte id minted with the face's slot tree at the founding's start. It is immutable and never reused. Rows that exist before the PersonaGroup does (the persona root, the founding's atom ask) seal under it, and so do the rows after, so nothing re-seals when the group arrives. The anchors row binds the fcid to the PersonaGroup. Rows are local and never cross vessels, so the id needs no cross-vessel stability.

The reds: the index of two founded faces carries no field beyond the entries' tree filename, group digest and ask digest and no byte of either face's name, handle, KEL prefix, root key or label, and reads the same whether the faces are locked or open; swapping two entries' trees makes an unlock open a tree and then refuse at the anchors check, with no face seated. The controls: the digests in the index are the digests the carriers on disk bear, the honest index seats its face, and `vault status` prints the count and the digests alone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/custody-derive-audit`>>

!! The custody root and the Keyhive archive: the owner audit

Wave D audit of every child the sealed custody root derives and of the vessel's Keyhive archive, read off the code (slice 2, ruling 7).

; The custody root's children
: ''One child derives today.'' The only call that reaches `holder.derive` is `custodyGateDoor` (`custody-seat.ts`), and its only consumers are `hearthSovereignPin`, `stoodSovereignPin` and the gate composition in `open-node-vessel.ts`: each derives the SOVEREIGN GATE BUNDLE (`GATE_BUNDLE_INFO`) of one Nexus and route. The registry (`ALL_DOMAINS`) declares other domains, and the door derives under any of them, but no other caller derives. The veil, Keyhive signer and hosting key named in the root's header derive from the vessel key, a face's atom or their own rows, never from this root.
: ''Owner: the node, not a face.'' The bundle keys the hearth's gate at a Nexus: the node's seat in that Nexus, apart from any PersonaGroup. It is a NODE-ONLY service secret and enters S as the separately named row `gate-root` (the custody root renamed for what it derives). It is never retained as a generic root, and no face derivation hangs from it.
: ''Stands:'' `gate-root` rests under the node's S key (the node route), and a derivation under any other domain refuses by name at the door.

; The face's Keyhive archive
: ''Owner: the selected face.'' Its atom mints one Keyhive Individual; its veil archive rests per PersonaGroup as `veil-archive-g<digest>` (D, ''face-hot''). A faceless place mints no Keyhive identity or archive.
: ''No identity above the faces.'' The daemon island restores only the selected face's veil from that face's FCK row.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/open-joints`>>

!!! The place seed's reach (ruled 2026-10-19)

* ''The veil leaves the seed.'' `deriveDyadVeil(vesselSeed, groupTag)` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/dyad.ts`) and `groupSecretOpenerFromSeed` (`packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts`) make the vessel seed an ancestor of every face's device veil and group secret. Each becomes D: the veil mints at random at the join and rests under that face's FCK; the opener reads the face's own key. The isolation red gains the row: a thief holding the place seed derives no veil and opens no group secret of any face.
* ''The veil stays with its atom.'' A face's veil rests under that face's FCK on that vessel and rides no one-face bundle; a restore onto a fresh vessel mints a fresh atom and never resurrects the dead one (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/identity-enacts-relation`>> #/identity-as-bond, Law 3). The one-face bundle's never-carries list gains the veil.
* ''The census sorts by capability, never by host'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). Each present use of a vessel seed, on node and browser alike, sorts by the capability it serves: a floor duty goes to the place signer, which a vessel holds only while it stands a floor; an act on a face's behalf (a dial, a join, an enrolment, a sibling channel, a daemon proof) goes to that face's atom; nothing else keeps a device-wide key. The browser's device seed (`open-browser-vessel.ts`: the Keyhive Individual, the oracle proof, daemon auth, sibling enrolment, the group-secret opener) and the node's (`open-node-vessel.ts`) take the same sort.
* ''The seed's other reach narrows to an allowlist.'' The online place signer serves the floor duties the roadmap's place-signer ruling enumerates; every other present use (the daemon's auth, the device-key signer, the internal asks) either names its domain on that allowlist or takes a key of its own.

<<~ ahu slot=`#/open-joints/the-node-tree-and-the-unlock`>>

!!! The node tree, the serving tree and the unlock (slice 3)

''The node (S) tree'' is the slot tree `s-slots.bin` wrapping one random S key. It seals the `gate-root` row (`gate-root.bin`). No face key, archive or serving key rests under it. The seal frame names it `lares-s-seal`.

''The tree bytes'' (S, serving and face trees alike, one keyslot shape): a plain JSON tree carries `keyCheck` (the keyed check of the one key its slots wrap) and `slots`, each slot `id`, `t`, `pins` and `wrappedKey`; the check derives under the domain `tree-key-check` and each slot wrap under `slot-wrap`; an S-sealed carrier opens with the magic `lares-s-seal`. These names land before the founding bake, so no earlier spelling is read anywhere.

''The serving tree'' (`sk-slots.bin`) stands on its own: the serving key (SK) wraps under its own slots, bound over the founding route with a wrapped key, salt and slot ids of their own, and no other key wraps it. A rotation of the S key moves no byte of it.

''The face tree'' (`faces/<fcid>/fk-slots.bin`) wraps that face's FCK under the face's routes. The three trees stay independent whatever route they share: a rotation or unbind on one leaves the others opening (`face-s-independence.test.ts`).

''One typed credential per unlock.'' `lares vault unlock` opens S and the face the composition mounts; `--face N` names the face; `--all` tries the credential on every face tree. A vessel holding several faces and told none refuses `face-not-named`. The credential reaches only the trees it fits; a face it does not fit stays shut.

''The sovereign gate.'' The daemon composes sovereign when S stands held AND the mounted face stands unlocked (a place with no face needs S alone); otherwise the floor composes. `sovereignStands()` reads this as a pure query. `lares vault lock --face N` drops one face; locking the mounted face, or locking S, stands the vessel back at the floor through the same gate, and a sibling's lock leaves sovereign standing. The founding sweep of seeds runs under the gate only.

''The first founding asks, with nothing preselected:'' whether persona zero's face tree takes the same typed route as the node tree ("one credential then opens both"), answered each time with no default. Either answer leaves the two trees independent.

''Status prints a face as one line:'' its handle index, a custody-id prefix, the count of rows under its key, and the group and ask digests the floor index names. A face's row never prints by name; only a fault does.

<<~/ahu>>

!! OPEN JOINTS


# ''Node private place signing seed'' (ruled 2026-10-19: a scoped online signer under a pre-rotated place KEL whose next key rides the founding card, built in the founding bake; the options it settled follow). Current `.vessel-key*.json` contains a private signing seed in a floor file and `loadVesselSigningSeed` reads it without a human route (`vault-carriers.ts:240-242`; `node-vessel-identity.ts:429-442`). ''Option A:'' keep a *named floor signer* owned by the node, protected at rest under an explicit mechanism; floor means no custody S key/FCK, while this narrowly scoped signer can support faceless Herm startup. Specify the protection, allowed signing/derivation domains, and restart behavior. ''Option B:'' put that signer behind an explicit boot custody door; public card/relay can stand before unlock, while operations requiring its signature wait/refuse until the door opens. Specify Herm boot and unattended container behavior. Neither option is selected here. The private seed is never F, never the current S key, and never the empty serving SK.
# ''Node archive row selector.'' The single `keyhive-archive` carrier is proven (`vault-carriers.ts:290-291`), but the path from each node provider/archive save to the selected face row remains unproved in the handbacks (round 1's YIN handback in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face`>>; round 4's YANG handback in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face`>>). Trace every node archive writer and restore reader and show an authenticated PersonaGroup selector before migration. Until then, the D target is a ''LEAD'' and bytes do not move.
# ''Tablet unattended sync.'' Public and ciphertext copy is F. Unattended D decrypt/transform needs a separate product ruling: route it to an authorized always-on node or define a narrowly named S principal, payload and boot door. No background worker receives an FCK by implication (`stable-founding-road.mem:207`).
# ''Guest-owned store.'' Prove whether the guest persists its own keys/rows. If yes, it is a separate vessel with its own D/F split, FCK trees, floor, and isolation red; if no, every private guest act remains under the host's admitted face session. Host keys never silently become guest keys (`stable-founding-road.mem:207`).

<<~/ahu>>

